0022-gpui-webview-cookie-store.patch (2026-11-15, rebuilt and compiled). Replaces the 2026-11-15 draft of the same
name (now in nsh-host-patches/superseded-2026-11-13/ alongside the other draft originals): that version was
written without the vendored tree present, invented a DESKTOP_HOST_CAPABILITIES const that does not exist, and
left every handler `call.reject(...)`. This version was built against the real tree
(third_party/nsh-host-build-0022, an APFS clone of nsh-host-combined-src's 0001-0021 tree) and a release binary
was produced from it.

Applies with `patch -p1` at the root of a copy of nsh-host-allspeech-src after the series 0010-0021, in order
(same convention as 0019-gpui-desktop-browser-bridge.patch). Verified: applies cleanly to a fresh archive of the
0001-0020 tree plus 0021, and `cargo build --release --locked --offline` of `nsh-gpui-host` with
`--features nsh-platform-gpui/webview` succeeds from it with no new Cargo.lock entries (every ObjC class/symbol is
reached through objc2/block2 raw `msg_send!`/`class!`/`extern "C"` statics, already direct dependencies of
`nsh-platform-gpui`, the same convention `native/capture.rs` (patch 0015) uses — no objc2-web-kit or
objc2-foundation feature additions).

gpui: webview cookie store (cookie read/write + cache clear via WKWebsiteDataStore.defaultDataStore())

Why: desktop parity audit H6 (DESKTOP_PARITY_AUDIT.md) — browser cookie import decrypts real cookies on the TS
side but had nowhere to write them (no WKHTTPCookieStore/WKWebsiteDataStore binding anywhere in this tree, and the
CDP shim's `Network.*` methods are stubbed with `{}`). Also needed by the audit's native-tab clear-cookies/cache
item (ipc/methods/preview.ts's resolvePartitionScope, clearCookies/clearCache).

Adds three `host.call` methods, listed in `desktop.capabilities`'s answer the same way patch 0010's dock/window
calls are (a host without this patch never lists them, so `desktopHostCapabilities().has(...)` on the TS side
keeps every caller a no-op):

  - "webview.cookies.set" { partition: string, cookie: ImportedCookie } -> null — builds an `NSHTTPCookie` from
    the cookie (`{ name, value, domain?, path, secure, httpOnly, sameSite, expirationDate? }`, cookieScope.ts's
    shape) and calls `WKWebsiteDataStore.defaultDataStore().httpCookieStore().setCookie:completionHandler:`.
  - "webview.cookies.clear" { partition: string, name?: string } -> null — every cookie (removeDataOfTypes: just
    WKWebsiteDataTypeCookies, modifiedSince: distantPast) when `name` is omitted, else only the cookies matching
    `name` (getAllCookies, filtered, deleteCookie per match).
  - "webview.cache.clear" { partition: string } -> null — removeDataOfTypes: the non-cookie website data types
    (disk/memory cache, offline application cache, local/session storage, IndexedDB, service worker
    registrations), modifiedSince: distantPast.

Known, documented scope decisions (not silently narrowed away):
  - `partition` is accepted but NOT scoped to. This host's web views (native/web.rs) are all built with the
    default `WKWebViewConfiguration`; nothing in this tree calls `WKWebsiteDataStore(forIdentifier:)`, so every
    tab already shares `WKWebsiteDataStore.defaultDataStore()`. Every call above acts on that one store. A real
    per-partition store needs a `websiteDataStore` plumbed through `WebViewHostProps`/`nsh-core` host props — a
    separate, larger change this patch does not attempt.
  - `httpOnly` on an imported cookie cannot be set: `NSHTTPCookie.cookieWithProperties:`'s dictionary has no
    documented key for it (`isHTTPOnly` is read-only, derived from a `Set-Cookie` response header, never from a
    dictionary an app builds). A cookie `webview.cookies.set` writes is never `HttpOnly`.

Built/verified: `cargo check` and `cargo clippy` (both clean) and a release `cargo build` of `nsh-gpui-host`, all
`--locked --offline`; a headless `nsh test` smoke run (new probe, not the existing workspace/combined-host) got
`webview.cookies.set`/`.clear`/`webview.cache.clear` all resolving `null` and `desktop.capabilities` listing all
three names. NOT verified: a cookie actually readable by a loaded page (no WKWebView was mounted in the smoke
test), or the deleteCookie-by-name path against a store that actually holds matching cookies.

